  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 157 PM CDT Sat Oct 17 2026\n\nHigh pressure this afternoon is sliding into Arkansas, bringing to \nan end our brief foray into cooler October weather. Breezy southwest \nwinds have developed, with temperatures this afternoon returning to \nnear normal levels after a chilly start to the day, which saw our \nfirst widespread freeze of the season outside of the Twin Cities \nurban core. MSP got down to 36 this morning, the first time MSP has \ndipped below 40 this fall, tying 1931 for the latest first trip \nbelow 40 in the fall on record.\n\nFor the rest of the week, we get into a \"stuck\" weather pattern. The \ntrough currently over the east coast will develop into a cut-off low \nin the western Atlantic as ridge builds over the top of it from the \nPlains to New England. This ridge will push the northern stream back \nnorth into Canada, allowing a very mild airmass to overspread the \nCONUS east of the Rockies. We'll have highs in the 60s Monday and \nTuesday, but from Wednesday into next weekend, highs in the 70s will \nbecome rather common. Fortunately, these warmer temperatures will \ncome with dewpoints in the 50s, which will keep us front having any \nsignificant fire weather concerns. The next chance rain has now \npushed off to next weekend, when a weakening cold front is forecast \nto make a run into the ridge. Given the forcing looks week and the \nantecedent airmass is warm and dry, I wouldn't count on these rain \nchances remaining in the forecast as next weekend moves into the 5 \ndays or less period. \n\nFinally, through the first half of October, the average temperature \nat MSP was 60 degrees. A full 7 degrees warmer than normal and 0.8 \ndegrees warmer than 1947, the current warmest October on record. \nWe're now getting into the \"cold half\" of October, which should \nstart chipping away at that average temperature, but there's very \nlittle in the way of cold air highlighted by the ensembles for the \nrest of the month and it may be tough for us to chip away at our \nlead enough to not finish in the top spot for warmest October on \nrecord. Something to watch over the next couple of weeks.",
